# Source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2" (solved), 2 d), and HoareLogic.pdf, "Empty else-branch".
id: empty-else
title: A conditional with a skip branch
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 d; HoareLogic.pdf, "Empty else-branch"
tags: conditional, skip, sequence, wp, consequence

task:
  The program sets $z$ to $0$ when $x = 0$, and to $1$ otherwise.
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, from the last statement back: first the two branches, then the
  conditional, then the first assignment. Then use the consequence rule
  to decide whether the triple is valid.

given:
  Condition B: $x = 0$

pre: true
post: (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  z := 1;             // i1
  {R3}
  if (x = 0) then {
    {R1}
    z := 0            // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    skip              // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch z := 0
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($z$) with the assignment
  expression ($0$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(z := 0, Q)
  = ((x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1))[z <- 0]
  = (x = 0 && 0 = 0) || (x != 0 && 0 = 1)     // every z is replaced by 0
  = (x = 0 && true) || (x != 0 && false)      // 0 = 0 is true and 0 = 1 is false
  = x = 0 || false                            // A && true is A, and A && false is false
  = x = 0                                     // A || false is A
set: R1 = x = 0

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch skip
rule: SKIP, WP
focus: else
text:
  `skip` does not change the state, so its weakest precondition is its
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)     // skip changes nothing
set: R2 = (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x = 0$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x = 0 : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x = 0) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if (x = 0) then z := 0 else skip, Q)
  = (x = 0 ==> x = 0) && (!(x = 0) ==> (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1))   // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = true && (!(x = 0) ==> (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1))                 // A ==> A is true
  = !(x = 0) ==> (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)                           // true && A is A
  = x != 0 ==> (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)                             // !(x = 0) is x != 0
  = x != 0 ==> x != 0 && z = 1         // when x != 0, x = 0 && z = 0 is false, and false || A is A
  = x != 0 ==> z = 1                   // when x != 0, x != 0 && z = 1 is z = 1
set: R3 = x != 0 ==> z = 1
text:
  When the condition is false, the skip branch does nothing, so $z = 1$
  must already hold before the conditional.

step: R4, the weakest precondition of z := 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($z$) with the assignment
  expression ($1$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R3$.
calc:
  wp(z := 1, R3)
  = (x != 0 ==> z = 1)[z <- 1]
  = x != 0 ==> 1 = 1       // z is replaced by 1
  = x != 0 ==> true        // 1 = 1 is true
  = true                   // A ==> true is true
set: R4 = true

step: P implies R4
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R4
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R4$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R4»
vc: true ==> true
why:
  $R4$ is $true$, and every formula implies $true$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The skip branch relies on the assignment `z := 1` before the
  conditional: when $x != 0$, nothing changes $z$ any more, so it must
  already be $1$.
